2.1 Decisions taken as given
Decision	Consequence accepted	How this design absorbs it
No central redemption orchestrator	Each channel builds its own saga machinery; no shared retry/compensation engine.	The engine is a library-shaped component inside this service (§7). It is small, generic and publishable as an internal Go module later if other channels want it — the migration from choreography to a shared engine stays additive.
Database per redemption channel	No shared order table; no cross-channel joins.	Cash's Aurora schema is authoritative for cash orders. Cross-channel questions are answered by redemption-status-svc from events, never by reading these tables.
Channel-specific process order is real	A single fixed pipeline would be wrong.	Step recipes as versioned data per subtype (§6.3), not per-subtype code branches.
Eligibility stays centralised	A synchronous dependency in the hot path.	Called once per order at a subtype-chosen point in the recipe; the decision is snapshotted locally for audit and never re-fetched.
