3.2 Internal architecture
Hexagonal, four layers, one Go module. The layering exists to keep the five rails from leaking into the aggregate — the single most likely way this service degenerates back into five pipelines in one repo.

Layer	Contains	Rule
api/	gRPC + REST handlers, request validation, idempotency intake, legacy facade translation.	No business rules. Translates wire shapes to commands.
app/	Command handlers, the step engine, the worker loop, transaction boundaries, outbox writes.	Orchestrates; holds no invariants. Never talks to a rail SDK directly.
domain/	The aggregate, value objects, the status state machine, invariants, domain events, the step vocabulary as types.	Zero imports outside stdlib. Knows that a disbursement happens, never how.
adapters/	Five rail adapters, ledger gateway client, eligibility client, Aurora repositories, SNS publisher.	One package per rail. A rail's quirks (SEC codes, cycle dates, void windows) stay inside its package.
3.3 Runtime topology
Process	Deployment	Responsibility
api	Fargate service, ALB, autoscaled on RPS	Accepts commands, persists the order and step-1 intent, returns immediately. Never performs a rail call inline.
worker	Fargate service, autoscaled on due-work depth	Claims due workflow runs, executes one step per transaction, publishes outbox events. Same binary, different entrypoint flag.
ingest	Fargate service, SQS consumers	Rail callbacks and files: ACH acknowledgements and returns, check clearing feeds, cycle-posting confirmations, posted-transaction feed.
scheduler	EventBridge Scheduler → SQS	Time-triggered steps: return-window expiry, stale-check evaluation, cycle-close sweeps, reconciliation.
One binary, four entrypoints, one Aurora cluster (own schema, own credentials). The worker is the only writer of workflow progress, which keeps the concurrency model simple: at most one in-flight step per order, enforced by a lease.

4. Domain model
4.1 Ubiquitous language
The five subtypes justify one bounded context precisely because they speak the same language. Where a word means something different per subtype, that word belongs in the rail adapter, not the aggregate.

Term	Meaning in this context
Redemption	A member's request to exchange a points amount for a cash-equivalent value through one cash channel. The aggregate.
Tender / Points	The points being spent. Denominated in points, converted at a rate captured at request time.
Hold	A reservation of points in the Ledger. Not a debit. Reversible by release.
Settle	Converting a hold into a permanent points debit. Happens once, after the cash side is judged final.
Release	Cancelling an unsettled hold; points return to available with no ledger movement recorded against the member.
Disbursement	The cash-side movement: a DDA credit, a statement adjustment, an ACH entry, or a printed check. One attempt against one rail.
Instrument	What receives the money: a deposit account, a card account, an external bank account, a mailing address.
Rail	The external system that effects a disbursement.
Reversal	A post-settlement correction: the cash came back (ACH return, stale check) so points are restored by a new compensating ledger entry.
Step recipe	The ordered list of steps a given subtype runs. Versioned data.
