4.4 Invariants
Each invariant names where it is enforced. Anything financial is enforced twice — in the aggregate and in the database — because a Go bug must not be able to double-debit a member.

#	Invariant	Enforced by
I1	At most one active (requested or held) ledger hold per redemption.	Aggregate + partial unique index uq_one_active_hold
I2	A hold is settled at most once; a settled hold is never released.	Aggregate state machine + hold state CHECK
I3	points × sc-camel-conversion-rate = cashAmount, rate frozen at acceptance.	Aggregate constructor; rate persisted, never re-read
I4	A disbursement is submitted to a rail at most once per idempotency key; retries reuse the key.	Intent-first write + unique disbursement.idempotency_key
I5	A posted card transaction is erased at most once across all open redemptions.	Partial unique index on erased_transaction.txn_ref
I6	Sum of applied amounts across erased transactions ≤ cashAmount.	Aggregate, on match acceptance
I7	Terminal statuses (completed, failed, cancelled) accept no further step transitions; only a Reversal may follow completed.	Status state machine (§5.1)
I8	Step history is append-only; an order's step sequence never decreases.	No UPDATE on outcome rows; optimistic lock on version
I9	Every status change emits exactly one status event.	Outbox row written in the same transaction as the change
I10	An instruction is immutable once the order leaves received.	Aggregate; instruction columns written once
4.5 Domain events
Internal domain events drive the local state machine; a subset is published outward in the shared Published Language (§8.3). The distinction matters: DisbursementAcknowledged is nobody else's business, RedemptionFulfilled is.

Event	Published	Raised when
RedemptionRequested	Yes	Command accepted and persisted; nothing has moved yet.
EligibilityDecided	Yes (outcome only)	Decision snapshot stored, approved or declined with reasons.
PointsHeld / HoldReleased / PointsSettled	Yes	Ledger gateway confirms the corresponding operation.
DisbursementSubmitted / Acknowledged / Settled / Returned	Submitted + Settled + Returned	Rail adapter reports a state change (inline result or ingested callback).
TransactionsMatched	No	Purchase Eraser matching step completes; carries matched total.
RedemptionFulfilled / Failed / Cancelled	Yes	Terminal outcome reached.
RedemptionCompensated / Reversed	Yes	Pre-settlement rollback completed / post-settlement reversal completed.
4.6 Alternatives rejected
Model	Why it was considered	Why rejected
Five aggregates sharing clients	Honest about differing process order; each type evolves alone.	Recreates the 11-service estate inside one repo. Five status enums drift; five retry loops; five audit shapes. Nothing is actually consolidated but the deploy unit.
One aggregate, one hard-coded pipeline	Simplest engine.	Wrong for ACH (verify instrument before hold) and Purchase Eraser (match before eligibility). Forces per-subtype if branches into the shared path — the failure mode the team already rejected at platform level.
Subtype detail in a single JSONB column	Zero migrations to add a subtype.	Loses NOT NULL and referential integrity on money-bearing fields; makes ops queries (“all unsettled ACH over $10k”) untypeable. Acceptable for step config, not for instruments.
Workflow recipes as Go code per subtype	Type-safe, no definition tables.	Changing a step order becomes a deploy; in-flight orders straddle two orderings with no version to pin to. Recipes as versioned data let in-flight orders finish on the definition they started with.
