Read the differences: Purchase Eraser must know the matched total before eligibility can judge the amount, so match_transactions precedes check_eligibility. ACH and Check verify the destination before any hold, because an unverifiable instrument should never freeze a member's points. Account Credit posts synchronously and therefore has no waiting step at all. None of that required a second service, a second status model, or a second retry mechanism.

5.4 Failure and compensation matrix
Failure	Retry policy	Compensation	Member-visible outcome
Eligibility declines	None	None needed	failed with decline reasons; points never touched
Eligibility unavailable	5 attempts, exp. backoff to 15 min	None	Stays authorizing; then failed as “try again later”
Hold rejected (insufficient points)	None	None	failed; balance changed between quote and hold
Rail submit fails deterministically (bad account, closed card)	None	release_hold	failed; points returned within seconds
Rail submit times out / unknown	Reconcile-then-retry with the same idempotency key	None until state is known	Stays fulfilling; never double-submitted (I4)
Rail confirmation never arrives	Scheduled poll, then ops alert at SLA breach	Manual decision path	awaiting_settlement with an ops queue entry
ACH return after settlement (R01/R02/R03…)	n/a — ingested event	reverse: restore points, record claw-back	reversed; points back, member notified with return reason
Check not cashed within staleness window	n/a — scheduled	Void, then reissue or reverse	reversed or a new disbursement in the reissue chain
Settle fails after successful disbursement	Retry indefinitely with alert	None — must not release; money already moved	Stays awaiting_settlement; hard alert. Losing the debit is worse than a slow status.
Member cancels	n/a	Allowed only while the rail attempt is voidable	cancelled, or refused with “already in flight”
