6. Database model
6.1 Options — where subtype data lives ADR-4 · open
One aggregate, one lifecycle table: cash_redemption carries the discriminator and everything the five subtypes share, and is the only table an ops query or a status projection needs to read. That much is settled. What is not settled is where the ~31 subtype-specific columns go, and the answer changes the schema materially.

One observation reframes the whole question. Roughly half of what looks like subtype data is not order data at all — it is rail progress: trace_number, effective_entry_date, posted_cycle_id, mailed_at, cleared_at, void_reason, return_deadline_at. Those describe the attempt on the rail, not the redemption, and belong on disbursement (which already exists, is already shared across subtypes, and already carries a reissue chain) plus a rail_detail jsonb for the genuinely rail-shaped remainder. Move them and the subtype residue collapses to three to five immutable, write-once identification fields per subtype — a token, a last-4, an account type, a rule pack. That is a value object, always read and written whole, never queried alone.

Option	Consequence	Verdict
A — Satellite table per subtype, 1:1 on the primary key (as drafted in Listing 2)	Five thin tables, each fully NOT NULL-constrained. One PK join on read. Rail progress duplicated between satellite and disbursement.	Viable, and the safest if rail progress is not moved. Five tables, five migrations and five repository branches for what is one value object.
B — One table, discriminator-guarded CHECK constraints + rail progress on disbursement	~12 nullable columns, integrity enforced conditionally: CHECK (subtype <> 'ach' OR (ext_acct_token IS NOT NULL AND acct_type IS NOT NULL)). No join. Fully typed and indexable.	Recommended. Keeps the integrity argument that motivated A — a guarded CHECK refuses an ACH row with a null token exactly as NOT NULL would — while collapsing five tables into zero.
C — Single JSONB instruction column	No migration to add a subtype. No constraints, no typed indexes; ops queries over instrument fields become JSON path expressions.	Rejected for instruments; correct for step config and raw rail payloads, where the shape genuinely is opaque.
D — Nullable columns, no constraints	Simplest to write; the database can represent an ACH order with no destination.	Rejected. This is option B minus the only thing that makes B safe.
Either A or B leaves the domain model untouched: one root, one status, one lifecycle, one repository, one transaction. The difference is only how the repository materialises a value object. The rule worth adopting: a subtype earns its own table when it has its own child collection or its own independently-mutable lifecycle — not merely extra fields. Exactly one of the five qualifies, which is why erased_transaction survives under every option.

