6.7 Hot queries, indexes and growth
Query	Frequency	Access path
Claim due workflow runs	Every worker tick	ix_run_due + FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT n
Load one aggregate by id	Per step, per API read	PK on root + PK joins on satellite, hold, run
Member's redemption history	Per app session	ix_cr_member
Idempotent intake	Per write	uq_cr_idem / api_idempotency PK
Match rail callback to disbursement	Per callback	Unique index on (rail, external_ref)
Unpublished outbox rows	Every publisher tick	ix_outbox_pending
Ops: unsettled ACH over threshold, aged	Ad hoc + daily	ix_cr_open + join cr_ach
Daily reconciliation against rail files	Daily batch	ix_disb_open, read replica
Growth and partitioning. The root and satellites stay unpartitioned — they are small and carry the unique constraints that make consolidation safe. The append-only tables (workflow_step, disbursement_event, outbox, audit_log) are range-partitioned monthly and detached to cold storage on a schedule: 13 months hot for operational tables, 7 years archived for anything that evidences a financial decision. Published outbox rows are pruned after 7 days.

6.8 Options — where workflow state lives ADR-6 · open
As drafted, an order's progress is recorded in two writable places: cash_redemption.status (the domain's state machine) and workflow_run.run_state + current_seq (the engine's). That is two sources of truth for one question — where is this order? — kept in agreement by discipline rather than by structure. Worth reconsidering before build.

Option	Consequence	Verdict
A — Separate workflow_run table (as drafted in Listing 4)	The engine's schema is self-contained and could be lifted into a shared module for other channels. Every step's outcome transaction writes two tables; status and current_seq can drift.	Viable, and the right shape if ADR-9 resolves toward a shared engine early.
B — Fold the run columns into cash_redemption: recipe_version, current_seq, next_run_at, lease_owner, lease_expires_at	One row, one optimistic-lock version, one due-work index. Drift becomes unrepresentable rather than avoided. The polling index moves onto the primary table — immaterial at 4 orders/sec.	Recommended. The run is 1:1 with the order, never queried without it, and always written in the same transaction.
C — Run state in a separate store (DynamoDB, queue visibility timeouts)	Cheap timers and leases; loses the transactional coupling between progress and the order.	Rejected. A step outcome that commits progress but not the aggregate is exactly the failure this design spends its complexity to prevent.
Paired with ADR-7: under option B, status stops being independently settable. Each recipe step declares the canonical status it enters (enters_status, §7.8), and the worker writes current_seq and status in the same statement — so status has exactly one writer and cannot disagree with progress. Step history stays append-only and is renamed redemption_step, since it was always the order's own log (Steps []StepExecution in §7.2) rather than an engine artefact.

