7.5 The step engine
type StepKind string

type StepHandler interface {
    Kind() StepKind
    Execute(ctx context.Context, sc *StepContext) (StepOutcome, error)
    Compensate(ctx context.Context, sc *StepContext) error
}

type Result int
const (
    Advance Result = iota // move to next seq
    Wait                  // park until WaitUntil or an ingested event wakes us
    Fail                  // per definition: fail or compensate
    Skip                  // not applicable to this order
)

type StepOutcome struct {
    Result    Result
    WaitUntil time.Time
    Output    map[string]any
    Status    *domain.Status // set when this step changes canonical status
}

// StepContext is what a handler is allowed to touch: the aggregate, the step's
// definition config, and a deterministic idempotency key for its side effect.
type StepContext struct {
    Redemption *domain.Redemption
    Def        StepDefinition
    IdemKey    string
    Now        time.Time
}
7.6 Worker loop and transaction boundaries
func (w *Worker) tick(ctx context.Context) error {
    runs, err := w.runs.ClaimDue(ctx, w.id, w.batchSize, w.leaseFor) // SKIP LOCKED
    if err != nil { return err }
    for _, run := range runs {
        w.pool.Go(func() { w.executeOne(ctx, run) })
    }
    return nil
}

func (w *Worker) executeOne(ctx context.Context, run Run) {
    r, err := w.repo.Load(ctx, run.RedemptionID)
    if err != nil { w.runs.Reschedule(ctx, run, backoff(run)); return }

    def := w.defs.Step(run.DefinitionID, run.CurrentSeq)  // pinned definition
    h   := w.registry.For(def.StepKind)
    sc  := &StepContext{Redemption: r, Def: def, Now: w.clock.Now(),
        IdemKey: IdemKey(r.ID, def.Seq, def.StepKind)}    // deterministic: no attempt no.

    // TX 1 — intent. Records that we are about to cause a side effect, so a crash
    // mid-call is recoverable and never re-submits under a new key (I4).
    if err := w.repo.RecordAttempt(ctx, r.ID, def, sc.IdemKey); err != nil { return }

    out, err := h.Execute(ctx, sc)      // the only place a side effect happens

    // TX 2 — outcome. Aggregate mutation + step row + outbox rows + run advance,
    // committed atomically on the optimistic-lock version.
    switch {
    case err == nil:
        w.repo.Save(ctx, r, out)
    case errors.Is(err, ErrUnknownOutcome):
        w.runs.Reschedule(ctx, run, reconcileDelay)   // Reconcile() decides next tick
    case isRetryable(err) && run.Attempt < def.MaxAttempts:
        w.repo.SaveFailure(ctx, r, err, backoff(def, run.Attempt))
    default:
        w.compensator.Start(ctx, r, err)              // walks executed steps backwards
    }
}
Boundary	Contents	Why it is drawn here
API intake (1 tx)	Root + satellite + api_idempotency + workflow_run + first outbox row	The order exists and is durable before the caller gets a 202; nothing has moved.
Step intent (1 tx)	workflow_step row in in_progress with its idempotency key	A crash between intent and outcome leaves a recoverable record with the same key — the retry cannot become a second payment.
Step outcome (1 tx)	Aggregate mutation + step row + hold/disbursement rows + outbox + run advance	Status and its event are never out of step (I9); the version check serialises concurrent writers.
Rail callback (1 tx)	inbox row + disbursement_event + aggregate mutation + outbox	Duplicate rail deliveries collapse on the inbox primary key.
Publisher (no aggregate tx)	Read pending outbox, publish, mark published	At-least-once outward; consumers dedupe on event id. Ordering per order preserved by partition_key.
7.7 A rail adapter, concretely
package ach

// Submit stages one NACHA entry. ACH is file-based, so "submitted" means
// "staged into the next window's batch" — the engine only learns of settlement
// through an ingested acknowledgement.
func (a *Adapter) Submit(ctx context.Context, r *domain.Redemption,
    idemKey string) (rail.SubmitResult, error) {

    ins, ok := r.Instruction.(domain.AchInstruction)
    if !ok { return rail.SubmitResult{}, rail.ErrWrongInstruction }

    entry := nacha.Entry{
        SECCode:       ins.SECCode,
        RoutingToken:  ins.ExtAccountToken,
        AmountMinor:   r.Cash.Minor,
        TraceSeed:     idemKey,            // dedupe key on the origination side
        EffectiveDate: a.cal.NextEntryDate(a.clock.Now()),
    }
    res, err := a.originator.Stage(ctx, entry)   // idempotent on TraceSeed
    if err != nil { return rail.SubmitResult{}, rail.Classify(err) }

    return rail.SubmitResult{
        ExternalRef: domain.ExternalRef(res.TraceNumber),
        State:       rail.StateSubmitted,
        // Drives watch_reversal_window; ACH returns can arrive days after settlement.
        ReversalDeadline: a.cal.AddBusinessDays(res.EffectiveDate, 5),
    }, nil
}

func (a *Adapter) Capabilities() rail.RailCapabilities {
    return rail.RailCapabilities{
        Voidable:        true,   // only before the file window closes
        AsyncSettlement: true,
        Reissuable:      false,
        ReturnCodes:     true,
    }
}
7.8 Options — how the step recipe is expressed ADR-5 · open
The recipe is the mechanism that lets five different process orders share one engine (§5.3), so how it is authored, reviewed, versioned and shipped is a first-order decision — not a storage detail. Four options.

Option	Change without deploy?	Consequence	Verdict
A — Database definition tables (as drafted in Listing 4)	Yes, by SQL or an admin tool	Rows nothing joins to, seeded by migration, edited outside code review. A typo in step_kind is discovered at runtime. To be safe in a regulated estate it needs an admin UI, a maker–checker approval flow and its own audit trail — none of which is built.	Only justified if a non-engineer genuinely authors recipes. Nobody does.
B — Declarative config in the repo, delivered by AWS AppConfig	Yes — config deployment, no rebuild	One YAML file per recipe version, reviewed in a pull request, schema-validated in CI and at load. Staged rollout and instant rollback come from AppConfig deployment strategies. Two artefacts (config + handlers) must be kept in step, which the startup validation gate below enforces.	Recommended. Config-driven where it pays — changeable without a build, versioned and reviewed like code, and typo-proof before it reaches an order.
C — Go declaration in code	No — requires a deploy	Type-safe, one artefact, reviewed alongside the handler it names. Retired versions live in a map until in-flight orders drain.	Strong fallback. Choose this if the team would rather not operate AppConfig; the cost is a deploy per recipe change.
D — External engine definition (Step Functions ASL, Temporal workflow)	Yes, per state-machine version	Retries, timers and visibility arrive for free. But workflow state leaves the service's database — a second source of truth, a second audit trail, and a direct conflict with database-per-channel.	Rejected for the lifecycle. Reasonable later for timers only if scheduler cost becomes a real problem.
# recipes/ach.v3.yaml — reviewed in a PR, shipped by AppConfig,
# pinned on the order as recipe_version "ach@3" at intake.
recipe: ach
version: 3
state: active                      # draft | active | retired
steps:
  - seq: 1
    kind: validate_request
    enters_status: received
    on_failure: fail

  - seq: 2                         # ACH verifies the destination BEFORE any hold:
    kind: verify_instrument        # an unverifiable account must never freeze points
    enters_status: authorizing
    on_failure: fail
    config: { verification: prenote, accept_cached_within: 90d }

  - seq: 3
    kind: check_eligibility
    on_failure: fail
    config: { rule_pack: cash_ach_v2 }

  - seq: 4
    kind: place_hold
    enters_status: authorized
    on_failure: fail
    compensate: release_hold

  - seq: 5
    kind: submit_disbursement
    enters_status: fulfilling
    on_failure: compensate
    compensate: void_disbursement  # only while the file window is open
    max_attempts: 3
    backoff: 60s

  - seq: 6
    kind: await_rail_confirmation
    enters_status: awaiting_settlement
    timeout: 72h
    on_timeout: escalate_ops       # never auto-fail after money has moved

  - seq: 7
    kind: settle_hold
    enters_status: completed
    on_failure: retry_forever_alert

  - seq: 8
    kind: notify_member
    on_failure: skip               # a failed notification must not fail a redemption

  - seq: 9
    kind: watch_reversal_window
    window_from: rail_settlement
    window: 5bd                    # ACH return deadline
Listing 5 — One recipe as declarative config. The five recipes differ only in this file; the engine, the status model, the retry machinery and the event contract are identical across all of them.
Validation gate — what makes config-driven safe
Rule	Enforced	Failure mode it removes
Every kind resolves in the Go step registry	CI + service startup	A recipe naming a handler that does not exist. The service refuses to start rather than stalling one order at step 4.
Every enters_status is a legal transition from the previous step's status	CI, against the §5.1 table	Config inventing a status path the domain state machine will reject at runtime.
Any step with an irreversible side effect declares compensate or is explicitly marked terminal	CI	A saga that cannot unwind because one step forgot its inverse.
Each config block validates against its handler's own schema	CI + load	A rule pack or window value the handler will not understand.
A version is immutable once any order has pinned it	CI check against a published-version manifest	An in-flight order's recipe changing underneath it — the single worst failure available in a config-driven design.
Exactly one active version per recipe	CI + load	Ambiguity about what a new order should pin.
Why this also answers ADR-9. A recipe file plus a step-kind registry is a contract, not a service. If Gift Card or Miles later want the same machinery, they adopt the file format and the step vocabulary and keep their own database — which is precisely the additive path from today's choreography to a shared engine, with no rewrite and no central runtime dependency in between.

