If matching fails midway, compensation releases the reserved transaction rows (released_at set) so the same purchases become erasable again — the one subtype-specific compensation in the estate, and it lives in one step handler.

10. Non-functional design
10.1 Capacity — stated assumptions
The volume figure available is value, not count: approximately $900M of annual cash redemption, Account Credit dominant. Counts below are derived and must be confirmed before sizing is committed (§12, OQ-5).

Assumption	Value	Consequence
Annual cash redemption value	$900M	Given
Average ticket (Account Credit weighted)	$60–$100	Assumed — the single figure most worth replacing with real data
Derived annual order count	9M–15M	≈ 0.3–0.5 orders/sec average
Peak-to-average ratio	8× (statement-cycle and campaign peaks)	Peak ≈ 4 orders/sec sustained, design headroom 50/sec
Steps per order	7–9	Peak ≈ 450 step executions/sec at design headroom
Rows per order (all tables)	≈ 20	≈ 300M rows/year across append-only tables → monthly partitions
The conclusion that matters: this is a low-throughput, high-value workload. A single Aurora writer with a polling worker fleet is comfortably sufficient — the design effort belongs in correctness, auditability and reversal handling, not in throughput engineering. That is also why a database-backed engine is preferred over a hosted workflow product: at 4 orders/sec the operational simplicity of one Aurora cluster outweighs the elasticity of anything larger.

10.2 Correctness, security, observability
Concern	Design
Exactly-once money effect	Achieved as at-least-once delivery plus deterministic idempotency keys plus intent-first writes. No step performs two side effects; every rail and ledger call dedupes on a key derived only from (order, step).
Concurrency	One lease per run, one in-flight step per order, optimistic version on the root. Two workers cannot advance the same order.
Ordering	Outbox partition_key = redemptionId on a FIFO topic: consumers see one order's statuses in sequence, without global ordering.
Audit	Every status change, decision and money movement is an append-only row with actor attribution. 7-year retention on the financial subset; operator reversals require dual control.
PCI / PII	Only vault tokens and last-4 fragments are stored — no PAN, no full account or routing number. Satellite tables are the only place instrument data lives, encrypted at rest, field-level masked in logs and in every event payload.
Reconciliation	Daily three-way tie-out: disbursement vs rail settlement file vs ledger settlement entries. Breaks land in an ops queue with the order id; the reconciler never mutates orders directly.
SLOs	Intake p99 < 300ms. Account Credit request-to-completed p95 < 60s. Status event published within 5s of a status change, p99. Zero tolerance on unsettled-after-disbursement beyond 24h — page.
DR	Multi-AZ Aurora, cross-region snapshot replication. RPO 5 min, RTO 1 hour. Recovery is safe because replay is idempotent: a re-executed step presents the same key and the rail refuses the duplicate.
11. Build and migration sequence
Phase	Delivers	Exit criterion
0	Schema, aggregate, step engine, outbox, status events — with one subtype and one rail wired end to end behind a feature flag.	A single Purchase Eraser order completes through the new path in a lower environment, event published and consumed by redemption-status-svc.
1	Purchase Eraser live behind the legacy facade — narrowest rule set, cleanest matching semantics, and it exercises the interesting ordering case.	5% → 50% → 100% of one client's Purchase Eraser traffic on the new path, reconciliation clean for 10 business days.
2	Account Credit — the volume type. Synchronous rail, no waiting step, so it proves the engine at real load.	Peak-day traffic served on the new path; legacy account-credit-post receives no traffic.
3	Statement Credit — first cycle-boundary settlement.	Two full statement cycles reconciled with no manual intervention.
4	ACH — first return-handling path, first watch_reversal_window.	Deliberate return-code drills (R01/R02/R03) reverse correctly end to end.
5	Check — void/reissue and staleness.	Void, reissue and stale-check paths exercised; escheatment rules confirmed with Compliance.
6	Facade retirement per subtype, nano-service decommissioning, cost/CVE reduction reported.	11 repos archived; Fargate baseline and patch surface measured against the pre-migration figure.
Ordering rationale: Purchase Eraser first proves the hard modelling claim (per-subtype ordering on a shared spine) at low volume; Account Credit second proves the throughput and money-critical claim at high volume. Doing them in the reverse order would put the largest dollar flow on an unproven engine.

